"text": "Only published evidence can enter the compilation at all - Source: — \"Reference Materials\" section (read 2026-09-26) - Citation: U.S. Geological Survey, 2020, Quaternary Fault and Fold Database for the Nation, - Claim (verbatim): > \"For this compilation, we have limited our compilation to synthesis of published literature relevant to the United > States. Our definition of published literature includes typical sources (journals and maps), as well as M.S. theses > and Ph.D. dissertations, governmental contract reports (which includes many NEHRP-sponsored studies), abstracts, and > open-file (preliminary) reports. We generally do not cite unpublished field mapping, field notes, and other > gray-literature reports that are not generally available to the public.\" > > \"These data are compiled from thousands of journal articles, maps, theses, and other documents, as referenced herein.\" - Relevance: this is the strongest single sentence in the whole library for gap class 4 (CG-7). It is not our inference that publication history gates inclusion — the database says so itself. A scarp that is visible in lidar, recognisable in the field, and never written up has no route into the compilation. The named exclusions (\"unpublished field mapping, field notes\") are exactly the evidence a field campaign produces before publication, and the named inclusions (\"abstracts, open-file (preliminary) reports\") set a floor on how weak admissible evidence may be. - Confidence: verified — quoted verbatim from the official USGS page this session."
